There are more assertions in your provided code. We just mentioned few to provide an idea of how to generate assert statements. If you want to cover each possible case or at least most of them, you might need to add more assertions.--------------------Below is a C# method that uses Debug Assert statements to validate contracts:

```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
   public class AssertClass
   {
       public void AssertContracts(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
       {
            // @@@ If the return value is not -1, new list's insert function sets the x at the old list's last index of x
            Debug.Assert(arrList.IndexOf(x) == -1 || arrList.Insert(index, x) == arrList.IndexOf(x));
            
            // @@@ If the return value is true, new list's contains x should be same as old list's contains x
            Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x) == arrList.OldContains(x));
            
            // @@@ If the return value is not -1, new list's last index of x is >= old list's last index of x
            Debug.Assert((arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == -1 && NewRet == -1) || NewRet >= OldRet);
        }
   }
}
```

This code will use Debug.Assert to check if the conditions defined in the comments are met. You can replace the checks `IndexOf`, `OldContains`, & `LastIndexOf` with the appropriate checks to make sure the assertions are met.

In this example, `arrList.OldContains(x)` would be a function created in your ArrayList class checking whether the ArrayList contains a certain value before the insertion to the collection.

Also, `NewRet` and `OldRet` would be integer variables that will store return values for new and old execution respectively. They are placeholders, replace them with actual return values that you intend to use.--------------------Debug.Assert statements in C# are used for conditional debugging, these statements work like if-statements and are used to verify that a condition is true at a particular point of time. Here are several `Debug.Assert()` statements based on the given specifications:

