Note: Since debugging assertions in software is useful for verifying that the program is behaving as expected, this kind of assertion should only be used during development and not in a production environment.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```
The code provided assumes that the old list's last index of x is greater or equal to the new list's last index of x. It's hard for pex to automatically decide all such situations, especially when using complex asserts, so the developer has to write this manually. The pex code above assumes that the latter is true, given the method declaration in the test, which is what this assert checks. 

If you use this assertion in a real-world project, and your code actually adheres to the contract, everything will run as expected. However, if you're using a framework or library that somehow misbehaves (and this code does not handle edge cases as needed), it could cause a potential problem. 

This is the power and potential danger of assumptions: it gives developers control over their code, but also means that you must understand exactly what conditions your code is required to hold for correct functioning.--------------------Here's how you can get those assert statements:

```csharp
using NUnit.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture, PexClass]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.Add(x);

            // Assert statements...
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Count - 1 >= Old_arrListCount);
            Assert.AreEqual(arrList.IndexOf(x), arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
        }

        // Similar assert statements for other methods
  
        [Test]
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            // ...

            Assert.AreEqual(arrList.Count, New_Ret);
        }
    }
}
```

